WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.360
Your kidneys filter about 10 grams of calcium a day.

2
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.820
You lose about a fifth of a gram in urine.

3
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.400
So almost all the filtered calcium goes back into the blood.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.960
What sets that last small fraction?

5
00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.780
Work it out with made-up round numbers, for a reference adult at steady state.

6
00:00:20.583 --> 00:00:26.383
In adult plasma, roughly half the calcium is ionized, about 40 % bound to protein,

7
00:00:26.703 --> 00:00:29.103
and about 10 % complexed.

8
00:00:29.483 --> 00:00:31.763
Calcium bound to protein is not filtered.

9
00:00:32.303 --> 00:00:38.243
A GFR of 125 millilitres per minute is 180 litres a day.

10
00:00:38.903 --> 00:00:44.083
Total calcium is 100 milligrams per litre, and 60 % is filterable.

11
00:00:44.083 --> 00:00:48.583
So the filtered load is 10,800 milligrams per day.

12
00:00:49.417 --> 00:00:52.097
Most of that is reabsorbed along the tubule.

13
00:00:52.717 --> 00:00:57.717
Roughly 60 70 % of filtered calcium is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule.

14
00:00:58.137 --> 00:01:03.957
20 25 % is reabsorbed in the thick ascending limb, largely between cells.

15
00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:10.237
The distal convoluted and connecting tubules take back roughly 5 10 % through cells.

16
00:01:10.817 --> 00:01:15.837
That small step is the one that parathyroid hormone, or PTH, regulates.

17
00:01:16.717 --> 00:01:20.557
Urinary calcium in the example is 200 mg per day.

18
00:01:20.977 --> 00:01:27.197
That is 1.85 % of the filtered load, so 98.15 % is reabsorbed.

19
00:01:27.577 --> 00:01:33.517
Now let reabsorption fall to 97 % with the same filtered load, as when PTH falls.

20
00:01:34.437 --> 00:01:40.277
Excretion becomes 324 mg per day, 1.62 times the original.

21
00:01:40.937 --> 00:01:45.917
A fall of about 1.15 percentage points moves excretion by 62

22
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.680
The parathyroid chief cells carry the calcium-sensing receptor, which reads ionized

23
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:57.180
calcium. A rise in ionized calcium activates the receptor and inhibits PTH

24
00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:02.320
secretion. The response is steep: a small change in calcium moves secretion a long

25
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.900
way. PTH raises active calcium reabsorption in the distal nephron.

26
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.060
So when PTH falls, distal reabsorption falls, and with the same filtered load,

27
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:13.520
urinary calcium rises.

28
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.440
Keep the rule. The kidney filters about 10 grams of calcium a day and reabsorbs

29
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:25.220
about 98 percent. The distal step that PTH regulates handles under a tenth of the

30
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.400
load. Yet a small change in that step sets the daily calcium lost in urine.
